{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Distribution charts answer where observations lie, how widely they vary, and\nwhether groups differ in shape or rank. The right encoding depends on whether\nthe reader needs familiar bins, compact summaries, cumulative probability, or\nthe detailed shape of each group."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-the-comparison","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the comparison"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reader question"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How often do values fall within fixed ranges?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Histogram"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do center, spread, and outliers compare across groups?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Boxplot"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What proportion of observations is at or below each value?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Empirical cumulative distribution"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do several binned profiles compare in limited space?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ridgeline"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do several mirrored distribution profiles compare?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Violin"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Must every observation remain visible?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A beeswarm or strip layout"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Binning, standalone quantiles, and density estimation are data preparation.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxX"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxY"},{"type":"text","value":" own their complete Tukey summaries because independently\nprepared quartiles, fences, whiskers, and outliers can drift. Dot collision\nplacement belongs to the chart because it depends on final scales, plot bounds,\nand pixel radii."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"preserve-observations-with-a-beeswarm","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve observations with a beeswarm"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a dodge layout when every observation should remain visible without moving\nits measured coordinate."}]},{"type":"code","value":"dot(rows, {\n  x: 'economy (mpg)',\n  key: 'id',\n  r: 4,\n  layout: dodgeY({ anchor: 'middle', padding: 1 }),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"52-beeswarm-dodge","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/dodge.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dodge Layouts reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" covers anchors,\nvariable radii, identity, and facets."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"inspect-frequency-with-a-histogram","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inspect frequency with a histogram"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A histogram groups quantitative observations into intervals. Keep thresholds\nstable when comparing revisions or groups; otherwise a changed binning decision\ncan look like a changed distribution."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"basic-histogram-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"basic-histogram","data-collapsed-indexes":"[]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { binX, defineChart, rect } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\n\\nconst responseTimes = [\\n  82, 91, 96, 103, 108, 112, 118, 121, 127, 131, 138, 144, 149, 153, 162, 171,\\n  184, 196,\\n]\\n\\nconst bins = binX(responseTimes, {\\n  value: (datum) => datum,\\n  thresholds: [80, 100, 120, 140, 160, 180, 200],\\n  outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\\n})\\n\\nconst chart = defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    rect(bins, {\\n      x1: 'x1',\\n      x2: 'x2',\\n      y1: () => 0,\\n      y2: 'count',\\n      inset: 1,\\n      fill: '#2563eb',\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear, axis: { label: 'Response time (ms)' } },\\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, grid: true, axis: { label: 'Requests' } },\\n  },\\n})\\n\\nexport default chart\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { binX, defineChart, rect } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst responseTimes = [\n  82, 91, 96, 103, 108, 112, 118, 121, 127, 131, 138, 144, 149, 153, 162, 171,\n  184, 196,\n]\n\nconst bins = binX(responseTimes, {\n  value: (datum) => datum,\n  thresholds: [80, 100, 120, 140, 160, 180, 200],\n  outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n})\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    rect(bins, {\n      x1: 'x1',\n      x2: 'x2',\n      y1: () => 0,\n      y2: 'count',\n      inset: 1,\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear, axis: { label: 'Response time (ms)' } },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, grid: true, axis: { label: 'Requests' } },\n  },\n})\n\nexport default chart","lang":"ts","meta":"group=basic-histogram env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/histogram/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open the catalog histogram"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor the complete vehicle dataset and update behavior."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The prepared rows should carry each bin's lower bound, upper bound, and count or\nproportion. Render those intervals with\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/bar-and-rect.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bar and Rect Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":" explains how the\napplication chooses thresholds and reductions."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compare-compact-summaries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare compact summaries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A boxplot summarizes quartiles, a median, whiskers, and optional outliers. It is\ncompact and comparable, but it does not reveal modes, gaps, or sample size on\nits own."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"15-boxplot","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass the raw observations to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxY"},{"type":"text","value":", or use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxX"},{"type":"text","value":" for horizontal boxes:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"boxY(morley, {\n  x: 'Expt',\n  y: 'Speed',\n  key: 'Run',\n  fill: '#bfdbfe',\n  stroke: '#2563eb',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The mark owns quartiles, 1.5-IQR Tukey fences, observed whiskers, outlier\npartitioning, and direct source lineage. Its tooltip datum discriminates\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kind: 'summary'"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kind: 'outlier'"},{"type":"text","value":". The "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/box.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Box Marks reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\ndocuments the exact statistics, styling, and interaction targets."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"preserve-every-rank-with-an-ecdf","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve every rank with an ECDF"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An empirical cumulative distribution shows the proportion of observations at\nor below each observed value. It avoids bin-width decisions and supports direct\npercentile comparisons."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"50-empirical-cdf","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a step curve because the empirical proportion changes at observations, not\ncontinuously between them. State whether ties share a rank and format the\nvertical axis as a proportion."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compare-binned-profiles","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare binned profiles"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a ridgeline when several prepared profiles need a shared quantitative axis\nand compact categorical baselines."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const profiles = normalize(\n  binX(episodes, {\n    value: 'imdb_rating',\n    by: 'season',\n    thresholds: ratingBoundaries,\n    outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n  }),\n  {\n    value: 'count',\n    by: 'season',\n    basis: 'max',\n    as: 'height',\n  },\n)\n\nridgelineY(profiles, {\n  x: 'x',\n  y: 'season',\n  height: 'height',\n  overlap: 0.78,\n  color: 'season',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"62-ridgeline-density","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"binX"},{"type":"text","value":" retains the episodes in each bin, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalize"},{"type":"text","value":" retains each bin as its\nimmediate source, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ridgelineY"},{"type":"text","value":" owns only the responsive category-step\noffset. This example is a normalized histogram profile, not a kernel density\nestimate. The "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/ridgeline.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ridgeline Marks reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\ndocuments overlap, category scales, curves, and interaction."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compare-detailed-group-shapes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare detailed group shapes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A violin mirrors a prepared normalized profile around each category. It can\nshow modes and shape that a boxplot hides. Its interpretation still depends on\nthe authored bins or density estimator."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const profiles = normalize(\n  binY(observations, {\n    value: 'body_mass_g',\n    by: 'species',\n    thresholds: massBoundaries,\n    outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n  }),\n  {\n    value: 'count',\n    by: 'species',\n    basis: 'max',\n    as: 'width',\n  },\n)\nconst summaries = groupBy(observations, {\n  by: 'species',\n  outputs: {\n    median: { value: 'body_mass_g', reduce: median },\n  },\n})\n\nviolinY(profiles, {\n  x: 'species',\n  y: 'y',\n  width: 'width',\n  span: 0.76,\n  color: 'species',\n  curve: d3AreaXCurve(curveBasis),\n})\ntickY(summaries, { x: 'species', y: 'median', span: 0.36 })\ndot(summaries, { x: 'species', y: 'median' })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"63-violin-distributions","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"violinY"},{"type":"text","value":" owns only mirrored category-step geometry. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"binY"},{"type":"text","value":", max normalization,\nand the median stay visible and retain source lineage. This catalog example is\na smoothed normalized histogram, not a kernel density estimate. The\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/violin.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Violin Marks reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" documents category\nscales, spans, curves, and interaction."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checks"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use counts when sample size matters and proportions when comparing groups of\ndifferent sizes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep thresholds and density parameters consistent across comparable views."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Show sample size or raw observations when a summary could hide sparse data."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supply exact values through tooltips, tables, or textual summaries; see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltips and Focus"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/accessibility.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use facets when each group needs its own complete distribution view. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/faceting-and-composition.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faceting and Composition"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Area channel details are in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/line-and-area.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Line and Area Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-comparison","text":"Choose the comparison","level":2},{"id":"preserve-observations-with-a-beeswarm","text":"Preserve observations with a beeswarm","level":2},{"id":"inspect-frequency-with-a-histogram","text":"Inspect frequency with a histogram","level":2},{"id":"compare-compact-summaries","text":"Compare compact summaries","level":2},{"id":"preserve-every-rank-with-an-ecdf","text":"Preserve every rank with an ECDF","level":2},{"id":"compare-binned-profiles","text":"Compare binned profiles","level":2},{"id":"compare-detailed-group-shapes","text":"Compare detailed group shapes","level":2},{"id":"production-checks","text":"Production checks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nDistribution charts answer where observations lie, how widely they vary, and\nwhether groups differ in shape or rank. The right encoding depends on whether\nthe reader needs familiar bins, compact summaries, cumulative probability, or\nthe detailed shape of each group.\n\n## Choose the comparison\n\n| Reader question                                            | Start with                        |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| How often do values fall within fixed ranges?              | Histogram                         |\n| How do center, spread, and outliers compare across groups? | Boxplot                           |\n| What proportion of observations is at or below each value? | Empirical cumulative distribution |\n| How do several binned profiles compare in limited space?   | Ridgeline                         |\n| How do several mirrored distribution profiles compare?     | Violin                            |\n| Must every observation remain visible?                     | A beeswarm or strip layout        |\n\nBinning, standalone quantiles, and density estimation are data preparation.\n`boxX` and `boxY` own their complete Tukey summaries because independently\nprepared quartiles, fences, whiskers, and outliers can drift. Dot collision\nplacement belongs to the chart because it depends on final scales, plot bounds,\nand pixel radii.\n\n## Preserve observations with a beeswarm\n\nUse a dodge layout when every observation should remain visible without moving\nits measured coordinate.\n\n```ts\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'economy (mpg)',\n  key: 'id',\n  r: 4,\n  layout: dodgeY({ anchor: 'middle', padding: 1 }),\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=52-beeswarm-dodge height=480 -->\n\nThe [Dodge Layouts reference](../reference/marks/dodge.md) covers anchors,\nvariable radii, identity, and facets.\n\n## Inspect frequency with a histogram\n\nA histogram groups quantitative observations into intervals. Keep thresholds\nstable when comparing revisions or groups; otherwise a changed binning decision\ncan look like a changed distribution.\n\n```ts group=basic-histogram env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { binX, defineChart, rect } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst responseTimes = [\n  82, 91, 96, 103, 108, 112, 118, 121, 127, 131, 138, 144, 149, 153, 162, 171,\n  184, 196,\n]\n\nconst bins = binX(responseTimes, {\n  value: (datum) => datum,\n  thresholds: [80, 100, 120, 140, 160, 180, 200],\n  outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n})\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    rect(bins, {\n      x1: 'x1',\n      x2: 'x2',\n      y1: () => 0,\n      y2: 'count',\n      inset: 1,\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear, axis: { label: 'Response time (ms)' } },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, grid: true, axis: { label: 'Requests' } },\n  },\n})\n\nexport default chart\n```\n\n[Open the catalog histogram](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/histogram/)\nfor the complete vehicle dataset and update behavior.\n\nThe prepared rows should carry each bin's lower bound, upper bound, and count or\nproportion. Render those intervals with\n[Bar and Rect Marks](../reference/marks/bar-and-rect.md). The\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) explains how the\napplication chooses thresholds and reductions.\n\n## Compare compact summaries\n\nA boxplot summarizes quartiles, a median, whiskers, and optional outliers. It is\ncompact and comparable, but it does not reveal modes, gaps, or sample size on\nits own.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=15-boxplot height=480 -->\n\nPass the raw observations to `boxY`, or use `boxX` for horizontal boxes:\n\n```ts\nboxY(morley, {\n  x: 'Expt',\n  y: 'Speed',\n  key: 'Run',\n  fill: '#bfdbfe',\n  stroke: '#2563eb',\n})\n```\n\nThe mark owns quartiles, 1.5-IQR Tukey fences, observed whiskers, outlier\npartitioning, and direct source lineage. Its tooltip datum discriminates\n`kind: 'summary'` from `kind: 'outlier'`. The [Box Marks reference](../reference/marks/box.md)\ndocuments the exact statistics, styling, and interaction targets.\n\n## Preserve every rank with an ECDF\n\nAn empirical cumulative distribution shows the proportion of observations at\nor below each observed value. It avoids bin-width decisions and supports direct\npercentile comparisons.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=50-empirical-cdf height=480 -->\n\nUse a step curve because the empirical proportion changes at observations, not\ncontinuously between them. State whether ties share a rank and format the\nvertical axis as a proportion.\n\n## Compare binned profiles\n\nUse a ridgeline when several prepared profiles need a shared quantitative axis\nand compact categorical baselines.\n\n```ts\nconst profiles = normalize(\n  binX(episodes, {\n    value: 'imdb_rating',\n    by: 'season',\n    thresholds: ratingBoundaries,\n    outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n  }),\n  {\n    value: 'count',\n    by: 'season',\n    basis: 'max',\n    as: 'height',\n  },\n)\n\nridgelineY(profiles, {\n  x: 'x',\n  y: 'season',\n  height: 'height',\n  overlap: 0.78,\n  color: 'season',\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=62-ridgeline-density height=480 -->\n\n`binX` retains the episodes in each bin, `normalize` retains each bin as its\nimmediate source, and `ridgelineY` owns only the responsive category-step\noffset. This example is a normalized histogram profile, not a kernel density\nestimate. The [Ridgeline Marks reference](../reference/marks/ridgeline.md)\ndocuments overlap, category scales, curves, and interaction.\n\n## Compare detailed group shapes\n\nA violin mirrors a prepared normalized profile around each category. It can\nshow modes and shape that a boxplot hides. Its interpretation still depends on\nthe authored bins or density estimator.\n\n```ts\nconst profiles = normalize(\n  binY(observations, {\n    value: 'body_mass_g',\n    by: 'species',\n    thresholds: massBoundaries,\n    outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n  }),\n  {\n    value: 'count',\n    by: 'species',\n    basis: 'max',\n    as: 'width',\n  },\n)\nconst summaries = groupBy(observations, {\n  by: 'species',\n  outputs: {\n    median: { value: 'body_mass_g', reduce: median },\n  },\n})\n\nviolinY(profiles, {\n  x: 'species',\n  y: 'y',\n  width: 'width',\n  span: 0.76,\n  color: 'species',\n  curve: d3AreaXCurve(curveBasis),\n})\ntickY(summaries, { x: 'species', y: 'median', span: 0.36 })\ndot(summaries, { x: 'species', y: 'median' })\n```\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=63-violin-distributions height=480 -->\n\n`violinY` owns only mirrored category-step geometry. `binY`, max normalization,\nand the median stay visible and retain source lineage. This catalog example is\na smoothed normalized histogram, not a kernel density estimate. The\n[Violin Marks reference](../reference/marks/violin.md) documents category\nscales, spans, curves, and interaction.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Use counts when sample size matters and proportions when comparing groups of\n  different sizes.\n- Keep thresholds and density parameters consistent across comparable views.\n- Show sample size or raw observations when a summary could hide sparse data.\n- Supply exact values through tooltips, tables, or textual summaries; see\n  [Tooltips and Focus](../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md) and\n  [Accessibility](../guides/accessibility.md).\n- Use facets when each group needs its own complete distribution view. See\n  [Faceting and Composition](../guides/faceting-and-composition.md).\n\nArea channel details are in\n[Line and Area Marks](../reference/marks/line-and-area.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Distributions\ndescription: Choose histograms, boxplots, empirical cumulative distributions, and violins for shape, spread, and rank.\n---\n\nDistribution charts answer where observations lie, how widely they vary, and\nwhether groups differ in shape or rank. The right encoding depends on whether\nthe reader needs familiar bins, compact summaries, cumulative probability, or\nthe detailed shape of each group.\n\n## Choose the comparison\n\n| Reader question                                            | Start with                        |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| How often do values fall within fixed ranges?              | Histogram                         |\n| How do center, spread, and outliers compare across groups? | Boxplot                           |\n| What proportion of observations is at or below each value? | Empirical cumulative distribution |\n| How do several binned profiles compare in limited space?   | Ridgeline                         |\n| How do several mirrored distribution profiles compare?     | Violin                            |\n| Must every observation remain visible?                     | A beeswarm or strip layout        |\n\nBinning, standalone quantiles, and density estimation are data preparation.\n`boxX` and `boxY` own their complete Tukey summaries because independently\nprepared quartiles, fences, whiskers, and outliers can drift. Dot collision\nplacement belongs to the chart because it depends on final scales, plot bounds,\nand pixel radii.\n\n## Preserve observations with a beeswarm\n\nUse a dodge layout when every observation should remain visible without moving\nits measured coordinate.\n\n```ts\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'economy (mpg)',\n  key: 'id',\n  r: 4,\n  layout: dodgeY({ anchor: 'middle', padding: 1 }),\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=52-beeswarm-dodge height=480 -->\n\nThe [Dodge Layouts reference](../reference/marks/dodge.md) covers anchors,\nvariable radii, identity, and facets.\n\n## Inspect frequency with a histogram\n\nA histogram groups quantitative observations into intervals. Keep thresholds\nstable when comparing revisions or groups; otherwise a changed binning decision\ncan look like a changed distribution.\n\n```ts group=basic-histogram env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { binX, defineChart, rect } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst responseTimes = [\n  82, 91, 96, 103, 108, 112, 118, 121, 127, 131, 138, 144, 149, 153, 162, 171,\n  184, 196,\n]\n\nconst bins = binX(responseTimes, {\n  value: (datum) => datum,\n  thresholds: [80, 100, 120, 140, 160, 180, 200],\n  outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n})\n\nconst chart = defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    rect(bins, {\n      x1: 'x1',\n      x2: 'x2',\n      y1: () => 0,\n      y2: 'count',\n      inset: 1,\n      fill: '#2563eb',\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: { scale: scaleLinear, axis: { label: 'Response time (ms)' } },\n    y: { scale: scaleLinear, grid: true, axis: { label: 'Requests' } },\n  },\n})\n\nexport default chart\n```\n\n[Open the catalog histogram](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/histogram/)\nfor the complete vehicle dataset and update behavior.\n\nThe prepared rows should carry each bin's lower bound, upper bound, and count or\nproportion. Render those intervals with\n[Bar and Rect Marks](../reference/marks/bar-and-rect.md). The\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md) explains how the\napplication chooses thresholds and reductions.\n\n## Compare compact summaries\n\nA boxplot summarizes quartiles, a median, whiskers, and optional outliers. It is\ncompact and comparable, but it does not reveal modes, gaps, or sample size on\nits own.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=15-boxplot height=480 -->\n\nPass the raw observations to `boxY`, or use `boxX` for horizontal boxes:\n\n```ts\nboxY(morley, {\n  x: 'Expt',\n  y: 'Speed',\n  key: 'Run',\n  fill: '#bfdbfe',\n  stroke: '#2563eb',\n})\n```\n\nThe mark owns quartiles, 1.5-IQR Tukey fences, observed whiskers, outlier\npartitioning, and direct source lineage. Its tooltip datum discriminates\n`kind: 'summary'` from `kind: 'outlier'`. The [Box Marks reference](../reference/marks/box.md)\ndocuments the exact statistics, styling, and interaction targets.\n\n## Preserve every rank with an ECDF\n\nAn empirical cumulative distribution shows the proportion of observations at\nor below each observed value. It avoids bin-width decisions and supports direct\npercentile comparisons.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=50-empirical-cdf height=480 -->\n\nUse a step curve because the empirical proportion changes at observations, not\ncontinuously between them. State whether ties share a rank and format the\nvertical axis as a proportion.\n\n## Compare binned profiles\n\nUse a ridgeline when several prepared profiles need a shared quantitative axis\nand compact categorical baselines.\n\n```ts\nconst profiles = normalize(\n  binX(episodes, {\n    value: 'imdb_rating',\n    by: 'season',\n    thresholds: ratingBoundaries,\n    outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n  }),\n  {\n    value: 'count',\n    by: 'season',\n    basis: 'max',\n    as: 'height',\n  },\n)\n\nridgelineY(profiles, {\n  x: 'x',\n  y: 'season',\n  height: 'height',\n  overlap: 0.78,\n  color: 'season',\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=62-ridgeline-density height=480 -->\n\n`binX` retains the episodes in each bin, `normalize` retains each bin as its\nimmediate source, and `ridgelineY` owns only the responsive category-step\noffset. This example is a normalized histogram profile, not a kernel density\nestimate. The [Ridgeline Marks reference](../reference/marks/ridgeline.md)\ndocuments overlap, category scales, curves, and interaction.\n\n## Compare detailed group shapes\n\nA violin mirrors a prepared normalized profile around each category. It can\nshow modes and shape that a boxplot hides. Its interpretation still depends on\nthe authored bins or density estimator.\n\n```ts\nconst profiles = normalize(\n  binY(observations, {\n    value: 'body_mass_g',\n    by: 'species',\n    thresholds: massBoundaries,\n    outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n  }),\n  {\n    value: 'count',\n    by: 'species',\n    basis: 'max',\n    as: 'width',\n  },\n)\nconst summaries = groupBy(observations, {\n  by: 'species',\n  outputs: {\n    median: { value: 'body_mass_g', reduce: median },\n  },\n})\n\nviolinY(profiles, {\n  x: 'species',\n  y: 'y',\n  width: 'width',\n  span: 0.76,\n  color: 'species',\n  curve: d3AreaXCurve(curveBasis),\n})\ntickY(summaries, { x: 'species', y: 'median', span: 0.36 })\ndot(summaries, { x: 'species', y: 'median' })\n```\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=63-violin-distributions height=480 -->\n\n`violinY` owns only mirrored category-step geometry. `binY`, max normalization,\nand the median stay visible and retain source lineage. This catalog example is\na smoothed normalized histogram, not a kernel density estimate. The\n[Violin Marks reference](../reference/marks/violin.md) documents category\nscales, spans, curves, and interaction.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Use counts when sample size matters and proportions when comparing groups of\n  different sizes.\n- Keep thresholds and density parameters consistent across comparable views.\n- Show sample size or raw observations when a summary could hide sparse data.\n- Supply exact values through tooltips, tables, or textual summaries; see\n  [Tooltips and Focus](../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md) and\n  [Accessibility](../guides/accessibility.md).\n- Use facets when each group needs its own complete distribution view. See\n  [Faceting and Composition](../guides/faceting-and-composition.md).\n\nArea channel details are in\n[Line and Area Marks](../reference/marks/line-and-area.md).\n"}
